B.W6=new A.de(0,18,"In early 19th-century Edinburgh, medical research was at the cutting edge of science.")
B.W1=new A.de(18,38,"Lecturers needed human cadavers for dissection, creating an immense, lucrative demand for bodies.")
B.W7=new A.de(38,64,"Resurrection Men crept into churchyards at night to dig up newly buried corpses and sell them.")
B.VU=new A.de(64,96,'Families invented "mortsafes": massive iron cages locked over graves to protect their deceased loved ones.')
B.W5=new A.de(96,125,"These iron cages you see before you are authentic survivals of Edinburgh's grave-robbing panic!")
B.J4=s([B.W6,B.W1,B.W7,B.VU,B.W5],t.M3)
B.VR=new A.kA("stop-a","Stop A","The Mortsafes","Head to the Mortsafes","Use the map below to find your way to Stop A. Look for the iron cages near the south side of the Kirk.","You should see these iron cages in front of you. Press play to hear the story behind them.","assets/audio/mortsafes.mp3","assets/images/screen-stop-a_photo.jpg",null,B.Ic,1,'In early 19th-century Edinburgh, medical teaching and anatomical research were at the cutting edge of science. But medical lecturers needed human cadavers for dissection, creating an immense, lucrative demand.\n\nEnter the "Resurrection Men" \u2014 grave robbers who would creep into churchyards in the dead of night to dig up newly buried bodies and sell them to anatomy professors.\n\nTo protect their deceased loved ones, Edinburgh families invented "mortsafes": massive iron cages or heavy stone vaults placed over fresh graves. These remained locked over the coffin for six to eight weeks until the body had decomposed enough to be useless for medical dissection, after which the cage would be removed and rented to another family.\n\nThese iron cages you see before you are authentic survivals of Edinburgh\'s grave-robbing panic!',B.J4)
B.u5=new A.j(0.35,0.1)
B.Mc=new A.j(0.45,0.45)
B.M5=new A.j(0.4,0.3)
B.M4=new A.j(0.36,0.18)
B.Hx=s([B.u8,B.Mc,B.M5,B.M4,B.u5],t.yv)
B.W3=new A.de(0,16,"Behind these iron gates lies what is often described as the world's first open-air concentration camp.")
B.VW=new A.de(16,38,"In 1679, over 1,200 Scottish Covenanters were imprisoned in this muddy field without shelter.")
B.W8=new A.de(38,62,"For four bitter months, with four ounces of bread a day, many perished from starvation and exposure.")
B.VV=new A.de(62,90,"Today, this enclosure stands as a solemn memorial to Scotland's turbulent religious history.")
B.HT=s([B.W3,B.VW,B.W8,B.VV],t.M3)
B.VS=new A.kA("stop-b","Stop B","Covenanters' Prison","Head to the Covenanters' Prison","Walk towards the northern section of the graveyard to find the gated entrance to the Covenanters' Prison.","Stand near the iron gates of the prison. Listen to the tragic story of what took place here in 1679.","assets/audio/covenanters.mp3","",null,B.Hx,2,"Behind these iron gates lies what is often described as the world's first open-air concentration camp.\n\nIn 1679, following the Battle of Bothwell Bridge, over 1,200 Scottish Covenanters \u2014 Presbyterians who resisted the King's attempts to enforce Anglican worship \u2014 were marched here and imprisoned in this muddy field.\n\nFor over four bitter months through autumn and winter, with no roof or shelter, they survived on four ounces of bread a day. Many perished from exposure and starvation. Others were executed at the nearby Grassmarket, while over 250 were sentenced to transportation to the Caribbean, only to perish in a shipwreck off Orkney.\n\nToday, this enclosure remains a solemn memorial to their sacrifice.",B.HT)
